  "physics": {
    "common": "Periodic cubic comoving box; redshift 9.375 from z_init=300. Identical low-resolution 1LPT and 2LPT displacement vectors, initial density fields, LCDM parameters, no smoothing, no FFTW wisdom, only density and LOS velocity_z. Source/heating switches, lightcone slicing, power-spectrum estimation and IC generation do not enter the requested timing.",
    "implementation_difference": "v3 computes and rounds box-fraction displacements in the float32 IC buffers, starts particles at (i+0.5)/N and assigns mass relative to coarse centers. 4.3.2 move_grid_masses uses double displacements relative to integer fine-grid positions and cloud-in-cell interpolation on the output index grid. At an unequal fine/coarse ratio, the deposition origins differ. The older C velocity-growth coefficient is float; 4.3.2 evaluates its coefficient as double. Both implement 2LPT and reconstruct LOS velocity from the evolved density. v3 also floors the fine/coarse address for its displacement lookup and multiplies/divides the six displacement arrays in place. 4.3.2 rounds the displacement lookup to the nearest coarse cell with periodic wrap and uses a common flattened index for all six values. The v3 native driver explicitly cleans FFTW and discards wisdom at the end of a node; 4.3.2 keeps global FFTW state in its driver. The time contributions of individual native CPU changes are not separated by this timing.",
    "comparison": "CPU vs GPU within the same version tests backend equivalence. Cross-version CPU vs CPU measures what the versions do with identical inputs and is not a portability tolerance test.",
    "radiation_density_precision": "The v3 OMr shown here is the C float read back into Python, while the 4.3.2 JSON is the Python double. The actual C float representations agree."
  },
